-- title:
--   A moduli place determines its Γ₀(N)-class uniquely
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field and $N$ a nonzero natural number whose image in $\kappa$ is nonzero. Let $x, x'$ be elements of `ModuliPoint N κ`, the quotient of the type of `Gamma0Pair N κ` — data consisting of a Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ together with a proof that it is elliptic and a point $g$ of its affine model with `addOrderOf g = N` — by the relation `Gamma0Pair.Step`, which relates two pairs when some variable change $\gamma$ carries the first curve to the second and the second generator equals $k \cdot \mathrm{vcInvFun}\,\gamma$ applied to the first generator for some $k$ coprime to $N$. Let $v$ be a place of `modularFunctionFieldFullC κ N` over $\kappa$, i.e. a valuation subring of that field, distinct from the whole field, containing the image of $\kappa$ and a principal ideal ring, the field in question being the intermediate field of the Laurent series field over $\kappa$ generated by the expansions `qExpand κ d (jqModC κ)` for the divisors $d$ of $N$. Assume `IsModuliPlaceOf κ N x v` and `IsModuliPlaceOf κ N x' v`, that is: for each of $x$ and $x'$ there exist a field $\Omega$ over $\kappa$ and a `ModuliTestDatum` for that moduli point over $\Omega$ — a place $W$ of $\Omega$ over $\kappa$, a Weierstrass curve over the valuation ring of $W$ with invertible discriminant after reduction, a point of exact order $N$ over $\Omega$ on it, a $\kappa$-retraction of the residue field of $W$ onto $\kappa$ exhibiting the reduced pair as the given moduli point, and a $\kappa$-algebra embedding `emb` of `modularFunctionFieldFullC κ N` into $\Omega$ whose values on the functions `jqNFull K N d hd` are the corresponding cyclic-quotient $j$-invariants — such that the valuation subring of $v$ is the preimage under `emb` of the valuation subring of $W$. Then $x = x'$.
--
--   This is the separation, or injectivity, half of the identification of the closed points of the modular curve $Y_0(N)$ over an algebraically closed field with classes of pairs (elliptic curve, point of exact order $N$): a place of the level-$N$ modular function field can be a moduli place of at most one such class. It is used, together with the complementary uniqueness statement, in the full-level constructions that attach places to Tate curves and compare rigid data attached to moduli points.
-- source:
